::CW_U02_L05_Q01::Passage: "I was definitely prepared for the speech, except for the missing note cards, the blank slides, and the small issue of forgetting the topic." What is the narrator effect?{
~A. Reliable certainty#Incorrect. The evidence contradicts the claim of being prepared.
=B. Irony through contradiction#Correct. The narrator claims preparation while listing evidence of being unprepared.
~C. Third person omniscient narration#Incorrect. The passage uses first person.
~D. No narrator perspective#Incorrect. The first person narrator has a clear perspective.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q02::Which sentence best defines satire?{
~A. A list of random jokes with no target#Incorrect. Satire needs a target.
~B. Any sentence in first person#Incorrect. First person is point of view, not satire.
~C. A scene with no criticism or contrast#Incorrect. Satire includes criticism or exposure of a problem.
=D. Writing that uses irony, exaggeration, or humor to criticize a behavior, habit, rule, or idea#Correct. Satire reveals a problem through crafted humor or contrast.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q03::Passage: "I did not lose the class pet. I simply allowed the hamster to explore leadership opportunities beyond its cage." What creates the humor?{
=A. The narrator uses inflated language to avoid admitting responsibility.#Correct. The narrator reframes losing the hamster as an opportunity.
~B. The narrator directly admits fault in plain language.#Incorrect. The narrator avoids plain admission.
~C. The passage uses no narrator perspective.#Incorrect. The narrator's perspective creates the effect.
~D. The passage is third person limited.#Incorrect. It uses first person.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q04::Which evidence would make a narrator seem unreliable?{
~A. The narrator admits uncertainty about another character's thoughts.#Incorrect. Admitting uncertainty can increase reliability.
~B. The narrator describes only what was seen.#Incorrect. Reporting observation carefully can be reliable.
=C. The narrator's claim conflicts with the narrator's actions.#Correct. A conflict between claim and evidence can make readers question the narrator.
~D. The narrator uses standard punctuation.#Incorrect. Punctuation alone does not create unreliability.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q05::Passage: "The new policy encouraged creativity by requiring every idea to fit into the same three approved boxes." What is being satirized?{
=A. A rule that claims to support creativity while limiting it#Correct. The contradiction is the target of the satire.
~B. Weather patterns#Incorrect. No weather appears.
~C. A character's private memory#Incorrect. The passage targets a policy.
~D. Figurative language in poetry#Incorrect. The passage criticizes restrictive rules.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q06::Which question best identifies a narrator's blind spot?{
~A. How many commas are in the passage?#Incorrect. Commas do not identify blind spots.
~B. What is the page number?#Incorrect. Page number is irrelevant.
~C. Does every sentence rhyme?#Incorrect. Rhyme is not narrator perspective.
=D. What does the narrator fail to understand about the situation?#Correct. A blind spot is a limit in the narrator's understanding.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q07::Passage: "Everyone admired my patience. That is probably why they all left the room after I explained the rules for the sixth time." What is ironic?{
~A. The narrator uses no evidence.#Incorrect. The repeated explaining is evidence.
=B. The narrator's claim of patience conflicts with evidence of annoying repetition.#Correct. Explaining rules six times suggests the opposite of the narrator's self-praise.
~C. The passage has no first person pronouns.#Incorrect. It uses my and I.
~D. The narrator is proven completely accurate.#Incorrect. The evidence undercuts the narrator.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q08::Which choice is a reliable narrator move?{
~A. Claiming to know every character's private thought without evidence#Incorrect. That can weaken reliability.
~B. Contradicting every action with a false claim#Incorrect. That creates unreliability or irony.
=C. Reporting an observation and admitting uncertainty#Correct. This shows the narrator understands the limits of knowledge.
~D. Exaggerating every detail to criticize a rule#Incorrect. That may create satire, not reliability.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q09::Passage: "Our school reduced announcements by creating a daily announcement about reducing announcements." Which method creates satire?{
~A. A literal weather report#Incorrect. No weather report appears.
~B. A private memory#Incorrect. The target is a school habit.
~C. A direct definition#Incorrect. The passage demonstrates satire rather than defining it.
=D. Contradiction and exaggeration#Correct. The announcement contradicts the stated goal and exaggerates bureaucracy.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q10::Which feedback best helps revise for narrator perspective?{
=A. Decide what your narrator misunderstands, then add evidence that lets the reader notice the gap.#Correct. This feedback gives a specific revision path.
~B. Make it better.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
~C. Add more words.#Incorrect. Length alone does not improve perspective.
~D. Remove the narrator from every sentence.#Incorrect. The lesson is about controlling narrator perspective, not deleting it.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q11::Passage: "I was not jealous. I simply counted how many times they laughed together for research." What does the narrator's perspective reveal?{
~A. The narrator is discussing weather.#Incorrect. No weather appears.
~B. The narrator is omniscient.#Incorrect. The narrator uses first person and personal interpretation.
=C. The narrator may be jealous despite denying it.#Correct. The denial and obsessive counting suggest jealousy.
~D. The narrator has no attitude.#Incorrect. The denial and explanation reveal attitude.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q12::What is the main difference between irony and satire?{
~A. Irony is always about weather, and satire is always about food.#Incorrect. Neither term is limited that way.
=B. Irony involves a meaningful gap; satire uses irony, exaggeration, or humor to criticize a target.#Correct. Satire often uses irony but has a critical purpose.
~C. Irony cannot appear in narrative writing.#Incorrect. Narratives can use irony.
~D. Satire has no target.#Incorrect. Satire needs a target.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q13::Passage: "The principal announced Free Choice Friday, then handed each student a list of the three choices they were permitted to choose." What is the satirical target?{
=A. A false version of choice#Correct. The scene criticizes a rule that claims freedom while tightly limiting choices.
~B. A character's favorite color#Incorrect. Color is not the focus.
~C. A natural setting#Incorrect. The passage targets a school practice.
~D. A reliable confession#Incorrect. The passage uses contradiction, not confession.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q14::Which line best shows a narrator admitting limited knowledge?{
~A. I knew exactly what everyone secretly thought.#Incorrect. This claims too much knowledge.
~B. Jordan was afraid because I said so.#Incorrect. This is unsupported.
~C. Everyone in the town had the same thought.#Incorrect. This overclaims knowledge.
=D. I could see Jordan's hands shaking, but I did not know why.#Correct. The narrator separates observation from uncertainty.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q15::Passage: "I only borrowed the trophy permanently." What creates irony?{
~A. The line has no contradiction.#Incorrect. It has a clear contradiction.
~B. The narrator uses third person.#Incorrect. The narrator uses I.
=C. The phrase borrowed permanently contradicts itself.#Correct. Borrowing is temporary, while permanently means lasting.
~D. The line explains all backstory.#Incorrect. It is brief and ironic.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q16::Which choice is the best target for school-appropriate satire?{
~A. A student's appearance#Incorrect. Satire should not attack a person.
=B. A harmless contradiction in a rule or habit#Correct. Satire works best when it critiques behavior, systems, or ideas.
~C. A private family tragedy#Incorrect. That is not appropriate for mild classroom satire.
~D. A random insult#Incorrect. Insults are not craft-based satire.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q17::Passage: "The narrator says, 'I never interrupt,' while interrupting three different people in the same paragraph." What should a reader infer?{
~A. The narrator is perfectly self-aware.#Incorrect. The evidence suggests a blind spot.
~B. The scene has no irony.#Incorrect. The contradiction creates irony.
~C. The narrator uses no perspective.#Incorrect. The narrator's self-view is central.
=D. The narrator may not recognize their own behavior.#Correct. The narrator's claim conflicts with actions.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q18::Which sentence uses exaggeration for satire?{
=A. The permission slip needed a permission slip to explain the permission slip.#Correct. This exaggerates paperwork to criticize overcomplication.
~B. The form was long.#Incorrect. This is plain.
~C. The form had twelve sections.#Incorrect. This may be factual but not clearly exaggerated.
~D. I signed the form.#Incorrect. This is plain action.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q19::Which explanation is evidence-based?{
~A. The narrator is unreliable because I dislike them.#Incorrect. Personal dislike is not evidence.
=B. The narrator seems unreliable because the narrator claims to be calm while describing panicked actions.#Correct. This uses claim plus evidence.
~C. The narrator is unreliable because the story is short.#Incorrect. Length is not evidence of unreliability.
~D. The narrator is unreliable because all narrators are unreliable.#Incorrect. That is an overgeneralization.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q20::Passage: "Our class celebrated Efficiency Day by spending twenty minutes voting on how to save five minutes." What does this satirize?{
~A. A peaceful natural scene#Incorrect. The target is a class process.
~B. A narrator's private grief#Incorrect. That is not the focus.
=C. Inefficient attempts to be efficient#Correct. The scene exposes a contradiction.
~D. A literal race#Incorrect. Efficiency is not a foot race here.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q21::Which narrator is most reliable?{
=A. I think Theo was upset because he left without speaking, but I am not sure.#Correct. The narrator makes an evidence-based inference and admits uncertainty.
~B. Theo was upset because I can read every mind in the room.#Incorrect. This overclaims knowledge.
~C. Theo was upset because I dislike him.#Incorrect. Dislike is not evidence.
~D. Theo was upset because the paragraph needed drama.#Incorrect. That is not story evidence.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q22::What must satire have to be clear?{
~A. A recipe#Incorrect. A recipe is unrelated.
~B. No evidence#Incorrect. Satire uses evidence, exaggeration, irony, or contrast.
=C. A target behavior, habit, rule, or idea#Correct. Without a target, satire becomes random exaggeration.
~D. Only insults#Incorrect. Insults are not the same as satire.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q23::Passage: "I was invisible at the party, which was convenient because I could hear everyone discussing the surprise they had planned for me." What is ironic?{
~A. The narrator knows every character's thoughts.#Incorrect. The narrator hears discussion, not thoughts.
~B. The party has no people.#Incorrect. People are discussing the surprise.
~C. The sentence has no contrast.#Incorrect. The contrast creates irony.
=D. The narrator feels invisible while actually being the focus of a surprise.#Correct. The narrator's belief conflicts with the situation.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q24::Which revision would strengthen narrator perspective?{
~A. Remove every attitude from the narrator.#Incorrect. Perspective includes attitude.
=B. Add a detail that shows what the narrator notices and what the narrator misses.#Correct. Noticing and missing details reveal perspective.
~C. Use only vague words.#Incorrect. Specific evidence is stronger.
~D. Explain the whole lesson in the scene.#Incorrect. Narrative should show the effect through craft.
}
::CW_U02_L05_Q25::Which checkpoint explanation would meet mastery?{
=A. The narrator says the meeting was efficient, but the scene shows three committees forming to reduce one form. The contradiction creates satire about overcomplicated systems.#Correct. This names the effect, target, and evidence.
~B. It is funny because it is funny.#Incorrect. This gives no evidence or target.
~C. The narrator is bad.#Incorrect. This is vague and judgmental.
~D. There is a paragraph.#Incorrect. This does not explain narrator effect.
}
